\begin{refsection}

\lecturepage{\_}{Orientation}{what this course is, what it assumes,
  and why a deployed model is where the work starts}
\section[Orientation]{Orientation}

% ======================================================================
% SEGMENT 1 -- what this lecture teaches.
% Source: Huyen, preface, "Who This Book Is For". The six reader scenarios
% become one frame each, and each is pointed at the chapters that answer it.
% ======================================================================

\subchaptergloss{the six situations this course is written for}
\subsection{Who this is for}

\begin{frame}{What this lecture teaches}
  \begin{columns}[T,onlytextwidth]
    \begin{column}{0.58\textwidth}
      How to take machine learning from an idea to a system that runs in
      production, and keeps running after you have stopped watching it.

      \vskip0.6em
      Machine learning here means \alertbf{deep learning and classical
      algorithms alike}. Logistic regression counts. So does a transformer.
      The course does not pick a side, because a production system rarely
      gets to.
    \end{column}
    \begin{column}{0.38\textwidth}
      \centering
      \phimg[1.0]{course_arc_idea_to_production}
    \end{column}
  \end{columns}
  \slidesources{Huyen2022}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{The scale this course assumes}
  \begin{columns}[T,onlytextwidth]
    \begin{column}{0.58\textwidth}
      The leaning is toward ML systems at scale: medium to large enterprises
      and fast growing startups.

      \vskip0.6em
      Systems at a smaller scale are less complex and \alertbf{benefit less}
      from the comprehensive approach laid out here. One model, one consumer,
      retrained by hand twice a year, does not need a feature store, a model
      registry, or a monitoring stack. Know which of the two you are building.
    \end{column}
    \begin{column}{0.38\textwidth}
      \centering
      \phimg[1.0]{scale_ladder_small_to_enterprise}
    \end{column}
  \end{columns}
  \slidesources{Huyen2022}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Written in an engineer's language}
  \begin{columns}[T,onlytextwidth]
    \begin{column}{0.58\textwidth}
      The vocabulary of this course is \alertbf{an engineer's}, because the
      problems are an engineer's: latency budgets, schema drift, on call
      pages, rollback.

      \vskip0.5em
      \begin{itemize}
        \item ML engineers and data scientists
        \item data engineers
        \item ML platform engineers
        \item engineering managers
      \end{itemize}
    \end{column}
    \begin{column}{0.38\textwidth}
      \centering
      \phimg[1.0]{roles_around_the_system}
    \end{column}
  \end{columns}
  \slidesources{Huyen2022}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{A problem lands, with raw data attached}
  \begin{columns}[T,onlytextwidth]
    \begin{column}{0.58\textwidth}
      The first situation you will recognise. You are handed a business
      problem and a lot of raw data, and it is on you to engineer that data
      and \alertbf{choose the metric} that decides whether the problem is
      solved.

      \vskip0.6em
      Picking the metric is the part that looks administrative and is not.
      Chapters 2 to 5.
    \end{column}
    \begin{column}{0.38\textwidth}
      \centering
      \phimg[1.0]{raw_data_to_chosen_metric}
    \end{column}
  \end{columns}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{The model works offline}
  \begin{columns}[T,onlytextwidth]
    \begin{column}{0.58\textwidth}
      The second. Your initial models perform well in offline experiments and
      you want to deploy them.

      \vskip0.6em
      Offline performance is \alertbf{a hypothesis about production}, not a
      measurement of it. Chapter 6 is how you evaluate the hypothesis,
      chapter 7 is how you put it in front of traffic without betting the
      service on it.
    \end{column}
    \begin{column}{0.38\textwidth}
      \centering
      \phimg[1.0]{offline_experiment_vs_online_traffic}
    \end{column}
  \end{columns}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Deployed, and blind}
  \begin{columns}[T,onlytextwidth]
    \begin{column}{0.58\textwidth}
      The third. The model is live and you have little feedback on how it is
      performing, so you want a way to \alertbf{detect, debug and address}
      an issue quickly.

      \vskip0.6em
      Three verbs, three different problems. Detection is monitoring,
      debugging is observability, addressing is a deployment path you can
      walk in minutes. Chapters 8 and 9.
    \end{column}
    \begin{column}{0.38\textwidth}
      \centering
      \phimg[1.0]{no_feedback_after_deployment}
    \end{column}
  \end{columns}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Manual, slow, error prone}
  \begin{columns}[T,onlytextwidth]
    \begin{column}{0.58\textwidth}
      The fourth and fifth arrive together. Developing, evaluating, deploying
      and updating models is done by hand for your team, and every ML use
      case in the organization was deployed with its own workflow.

      \vskip0.6em
      What you want is \alertbf{one shared foundation}: a model store, a
      feature store, monitoring tools, reused across use cases instead of
      rebuilt per team. Chapters 9 and 10.
    \end{column}
    \begin{column}{0.38\textwidth}
      \centering
      \phimg[1.0]{per_usecase_workflows_vs_platform}
    \end{column}
  \end{columns}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Responsibility is not a separate module}
  \begin{columns}[T,onlytextwidth]
    \begin{column}{0.58\textwidth}
      The sixth. You suspect there are biases in your ML systems and you want
      to make those systems responsible.

      \vskip0.6em
      That is chapter 11, the last chapter and not the optional one. ML
      systems are built by people, for people, and \alertbf{can have outsized
      impact on the life of many}.
    \end{column}
    \begin{column}{0.38\textwidth}
      \centering
      \phimg[1.0]{bias_audit_of_a_live_system}
    \end{column}
  \end{columns}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Also for you if}
  \begin{columns}[T,onlytextwidth]
    \begin{column}{0.58\textwidth}
      \begin{itemize}
        \item you build tools and want to find the underserved areas of ML
          production, and where your tool sits in that ecosystem
        \item you are looking for an ML role in industry
        \item you lead a team or a business and are weighing whether to adopt
          ML in a product or a process
      \end{itemize}
      \vskip0.5em
      Without a strong technical background, the value is concentrated in
      \alertbf{chapters 1, 2 and 11}.
    \end{column}
    \begin{column}{0.38\textwidth}
      \centering
      \phimg[1.0]{three_other_audiences}
    \end{column}
  \end{columns}
  \slidesources{Huyen2022}
\end{frame}

% ======================================================================
% SEGMENT 2 -- what this lecture is not.
% Source: Huyen, preface, "What This Book Is Not". The first frame is
% deliberately the short one; the prerequisite list is its own material and
% would bury the boundary if it shared a slide with it.
% ======================================================================

\subchaptergloss{the boundary, and what sits on the other side of it}
\subsection{What this is not}

\begin{frame}{What this lecture is not}
  \begin{columns}[T,onlytextwidth]
    \begin{column}{0.58\textwidth}
      Not an introduction to machine learning. The theory has books, courses
      and resources enough, and this course starts where they stop.

      \vskip0.8em
      Not a tutorial. \alertbf{Few code snippets, many trade-offs.}
    \end{column}
    \begin{column}{0.38\textwidth}
      \centering
      \phimg[1.0]{in_scope_out_of_scope}
    \end{column}
  \end{columns}
  \slidesources{Huyen2022}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Assumed: models and techniques}
  \begin{columns}[T,onlytextwidth]
    \begin{column}{0.58\textwidth}
      You need \alertbf{a rough sense} of these, not command of them.

      \vskip0.4em
      \begin{itemize}
        \item models: clustering, logistic regression, decision trees,
          collaborative filtering, and neural network architectures
          including feed-forward, recurrent, convolutional and transformer
        \item techniques: supervised versus unsupervised, gradient descent,
          objective and loss functions, regularization, generalization,
          hyperparameter tuning
      \end{itemize}
    \end{column}
    \begin{column}{0.38\textwidth}
      \centering
      \phimg[1.0]{prerequisite_models_map}
    \end{column}
  \end{columns}
  \slidesources{Huyen2022}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Assumed: metrics, statistics, tasks}
  \begin{columns}[T,onlytextwidth]
    \begin{column}{0.58\textwidth}
      \begin{itemize}
        \item metrics: accuracy, F1, precision, recall, ROC, mean squared
          error, log-likelihood
        \item statistics: variance, probability, normal and long-tail
          distributions
        \item tasks: language modeling, anomaly detection, object
          classification, machine translation
      \end{itemize}
      \vskip0.4em
      Where the exact definition takes effort to recall, F1 being the usual
      one, \alertbf{a margin note hands it back to you} at the point of use.
    \end{column}
    \begin{column}{0.38\textwidth}
      \centering
      \phimg[1.0]{prerequisite_metrics_checklist}
    \end{column}
  \end{columns}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Tools go out of style, problems do not}
  \begin{columns}[T,onlytextwidth]
    \begin{column}{0.58\textwidth}
      Tools appear in this course to illustrate a concept, never as the
      lesson. Technologies evolve, tools go in and out of style quickly, and
      \alertbf{fundamental approaches to problem solving last longer}.

      \vskip0.6em
      What you get instead is a framework for judging which tool fits your
      case. Once you have picked one, its tutorial is a search away.
    \end{column}
    \begin{column}{0.38\textwidth}
      \centering
      \phimg[1.0]{tool_churn_timeline}
    \end{column}
  \end{columns}
  \slidesources{Huyen2022,Lakshmanan2020}
\end{frame}

% ======================================================================
% SEGMENT 3 -- navigating this lecture.
% Source: Huyen, preface, "Navigating This Book". One frame per chapter
% grouping, in the book's own grouping, plus the two asides the section
% makes: why data systems come early, and what "data scientist" means here.
% ======================================================================

\subchaptergloss{the order of the chapters is the order in which the problems
  arrive}
\subsection{Navigating this lecture}

\begin{frame}{Navigating this lecture}
  \begin{withmargin}
    \begin{tdmain}
      \centering
      \phimg[0.92]{course_map_eleven_chapters}
    \end{tdmain}
    \begin{tdmargin}
      Eleven chapters, one lecture each, ordered by the
      \alertbf{lifecycle of an ML project} rather than by the taxonomy of the
      field. Each chapter is the problem you meet next, not the next topic in
      a syllabus.
    \end{tdmargin}
  \end{withmargin}
  \slidesources{Huyen2022}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Chapters 1 and 2: the groundwork}
  \begin{columns}[T,onlytextwidth]
    \begin{column}{0.58\textwidth}
      The two chapters that set a project up for success, starting from the
      most basic question there is: \alertbf{does your project need ML at
      all?}

      \vskip0.6em
      Then choosing the objectives, and framing the problem so the solution
      gets simpler. Already familiar with all of this and impatient for the
      technical part? These two are the skippable ones.
    \end{column}
    \begin{column}{0.38\textwidth}
      \centering
      \phimg[1.0]{groundwork_does_it_need_ml}
    \end{column}
  \end{columns}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Chapters 4 to 6: before deployment}
  \begin{columns}[T,onlytextwidth]
    \begin{column}{0.58\textwidth}
      Creating the training data, engineering the features, developing and
      evaluating models in a development environment.

      \vskip0.6em
      This is the phase where \alertbf{ML expertise and domain expertise are
      needed at the same time}. Neither one alone gets a usable label
      definition out of a subject matter expert.
    \end{column}
    \begin{column}{0.38\textwidth}
      \centering
      \phimg[1.0]{predeployment_data_features_models}
    \end{column}
  \end{columns}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Chapters 7 to 9: deployment, and after}
  \begin{columns}[T,onlytextwidth]
    \begin{column}{0.58\textwidth}
      Having a model deployed is not the end of the deployment process.

      \vskip0.6em
      The deployed model has to be monitored and continually updated as
      \alertbf{the environment and the business requirements change}
      underneath it. Chapters 8 and 9 are that story, told through a failure
      most practitioners will recognise.
    \end{column}
    \begin{column}{0.38\textwidth}
      \centering
      \phimg[1.0]{post_deployment_loop}
    \end{column}
  \end{columns}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Chapters 3 and 10: the layer underneath}
  \begin{columns}[T,onlytextwidth]
    \begin{column}{0.58\textwidth}
      Infrastructure is what lets stakeholders from different backgrounds
      deliver one system together.

      \vskip0.6em
      Chapter 3 is data systems: databases, data formats, data movement,
      processing engines. Chapter 10 is \alertbf{compute infrastructure and
      ML platforms}. They sit at opposite ends of the book because they sit
      under opposite ends of the system.
    \end{column}
    \begin{column}{0.38\textwidth}
      \centering
      \phimg[1.0]{infrastructure_data_and_compute}
    \end{column}
  \end{columns}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Why data systems come this early}
  \begin{columns}[T,onlytextwidth]
    \begin{column}{0.58\textwidth}
      Data systems are sparsely covered in ML coursework, so many data
      scientists file them under low level, or under someone else's job.

      \vskip0.6em
      An ML system \alertbf{depends on data}. Covering the basics in chapter
      3 is what puts the room on the same page for every argument about data
      that the remaining eight chapters have.
    \end{column}
    \begin{column}{0.38\textwidth}
      \centering
      \phimg[1.0]{data_systems_before_models}
    \end{column}
  \end{columns}
  \slidesources{Kleppmann2017}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Chapter 11: the human side}
  \begin{columns}[T,onlytextwidth]
    \begin{column}{0.58\textwidth}
      ML systems are built by people, for people, and can have outsized
      impact on the life of many. A course on ML in production that stopped
      at the technical aspects would stop early.

      \vskip0.6em
      The last chapter covers the user experience the system produces, how a
      team divides this work, and \alertbf{responsible AI}.
    \end{column}
    \begin{column}{0.38\textwidth}
      \centering
      \phimg[1.0]{people_build_it_people_use_it}
    \end{column}
  \end{columns}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{``Data scientist'' is an umbrella term here}
  \begin{columns}[T,onlytextwidth]
    \begin{column}{0.58\textwidth}
      The role has evolved a lot in the last few years and what it should
      entail is still being argued about. Chapter 10 goes into some of those
      arguments.

      \vskip0.6em
      In this course the term covers \alertbf{anyone who develops and deploys
      ML models}, whatever the job title says: ML engineer, data engineer,
      data analyst.
    \end{column}
    \begin{column}{0.38\textwidth}
      \centering
      \phimg[1.0]{job_titles_one_job}
    \end{column}
  \end{columns}
  \slidesources{Huyen2022,Amershi2019}
\end{frame}

% ======================================================================
% SEGMENT 4 -- the motivation.
% Source: Huyen, chapter 1, everything before the heading "When to Use
% Machine Learning". ch01 opens on exactly that heading, so this segment
% hands over to it without repeating a line of it.
% ======================================================================

\subchaptergloss{November 2016, the five years after it, and the small box in
  the middle}
\subsection{Why any of this}

\begin{frame}{November 2016}
  \begin{columns}[T,onlytextwidth]
    \begin{column}{0.58\textwidth}
      Google announced that its multilingual neural machine translation
      system had gone into Google Translate: one of the \alertbf{first
      success stories of deep neural networks in production at scale}.

      \vskip0.6em
      By Google's own account, translation quality improved more in that
      single leap than in the previous ten years combined.
    \end{column}
    \begin{column}{0.38\textwidth}
      \centering
      \phimg[1.0]{google_translate_nmt_2016}
    \end{column}
  \end{columns}
  \slidesources{Huyen2022}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Five years later}
  \begin{columns}[T,onlytextwidth]
    \begin{column}{0.58\textwidth}
      That success renewed interest in machine learning at large, and more
      and more companies turned to it for their hardest problems.

      \vskip0.6em
      In five years ML reached how we access information, how we communicate,
      how we work, \alertbf{how we find love}. The spread has been fast enough
      that life without it is already hard to picture.
    \end{column}
    \begin{column}{0.38\textwidth}
      \centering
      \phimg[1.0]{ml_in_daily_life}
    \end{column}
  \end{columns}
  \slidesources{Huyen2022}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{And mostly still ahead}
  \begin{columns}[T,onlytextwidth]
    \begin{column}{0.58\textwidth}
      Many more use cases are waiting to be explored: health care,
      transportation, farming, and helping us understand the universe.

      \vskip0.6em
      What stands between those fields and a working system is rarely the
      algorithm. \alertbf{It is everything around the algorithm}, and that is
      what the next eleven lectures are.
    \end{column}
    \begin{column}{0.38\textwidth}
      \centering
      \phimg[1.0]{open_fields_health_transport_farming}
    \end{column}
  \end{columns}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{What people picture}
  \begin{columns}[T,onlytextwidth]
    \begin{column}{0.58\textwidth}
      Say ``machine learning system'' and most people picture the algorithms
      being used: logistic regression, or some architecture of neural
      network.

      \vskip0.8em
      \alertbf{The algorithm is only a small part of an ML system in
      production.}
    \end{column}
    \begin{column}{0.38\textwidth}
      \centering
      \phimg[1.0]{what_people_picture_just_the_model}
    \end{column}
  \end{columns}
  \slidesources{Huyen2022,Sculley2015}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{What is actually in there}
  \begin{columns}[T,onlytextwidth]
    \begin{column}{0.58\textwidth}
      \begin{itemize}
        \item the business requirements that gave birth to the project
        \item the interface where users and developers meet the system
        \item the data stack
        \item the logic for developing, monitoring and updating the models
        \item the infrastructure that delivers that logic
      \end{itemize}
      \vskip0.4em
      Six components, and \alertbf{five of them are not the model}.
    \end{column}
    \begin{column}{0.38\textwidth}
      \centering
      \phimg[1.0]{system_components_stacked}
    \end{column}
  \end{columns}
  \slidesources{Huyen2022,Sculley2015}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Anatomy of an ML system}
  \begin{withmargin}
    \begin{tdmain}
      \centering
      \phimg[0.92]{anatomy_of_an_ml_system}
    \end{tdmain}
    \begin{tdmargin}
      Figure 1-1 of the book, redrawn dark. Every box on it is a chapter of
      this course, which is also how you read the map: the chapter numbers
      are printed on the components they cover.

      \vskip0.6em
      \alertbf{ML algorithms} is the small box in the middle.
    \end{tdmargin}
  \end{withmargin}
  \slidesources{Huyen2022}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{MLOps and ML systems design}
  \begin{columns}[T,onlytextwidth]
    \begin{column}{0.58\textwidth}
      \small
      \begin{tdblock}
        \textbf{MLOps.} Ops comes from DevOps, Developments and Operations.
        To operationalize is to bring into production: deploy, monitor,
        maintain. MLOps is the set of tools and best practices for bringing
        ML into production.
      \end{tdblock}
      \vskip0.3em
      \begin{tddef}{ML systems design}
        The system approach to MLOps: the whole system at once, so that every
        component and every stakeholder can meet the specified objectives and
        requirements.
      \end{tddef}
    \end{column}
    \begin{column}{0.38\textwidth}
      \centering
      \phimg[1.0]{mlops_inside_systems_design}
    \end{column}
  \end{columns}
  \slidesources{Huyen2022,Zinkevich2017}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Not a course about algorithms}
  \begin{columns}[T,onlytextwidth]
    \begin{column}{0.58\textwidth}
      There are excellent books about individual ML algorithms. This course
      is not one of them, and covers none of them in detail.

      \vskip0.6em
      Algorithms become outdated quickly as new ones are developed.
      \alertbf{The framework should still fit the new ones}: a way to build
      the solution that works for your problem, regardless of which algorithm
      you end up using.
    \end{column}
    \begin{column}{0.38\textwidth}
      \centering
      \phimg[1.0]{framework_outlives_the_algorithm}
    \end{column}
  \end{columns}
  \slidesources{Huyen2022,Breck2017}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{What Lecture I does}
  \begin{columns}[T,onlytextwidth]
    \begin{column}{0.58\textwidth}
      It asks the question that comes before every other question in the
      course: \alertbf{when to use machine learning, and when not to}. Then
      it walks the popular use cases, so the answer stops being abstract.

      \vskip0.6em
      After the use cases come the two comparisons that explain why
      production is hard: ML in research versus ML in production, and ML
      systems versus traditional software.
    \end{column}
    \begin{column}{0.38\textwidth}
      \centering
      \phimg[1.0]{lecture_one_route}
    \end{column}
  \end{columns}
  \slidesources{Huyen2022}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}{Which of the two are you?}
  \begin{columns}[T,onlytextwidth]
    \begin{column}{0.58\textwidth}
      If your experience with ML has been academic, the next chapter gives an
      honest view of ML in the real world, and \alertbf{sets your first
      application up for success}.

      \vskip0.6em
      If you have already been in the trenches of developing applied ML
      systems, much of it will be familiar. Read it as a checklist instead,
      and mark the lines your last project skipped.
    \end{column}
    \begin{column}{0.38\textwidth}
      \centering
      \phimg[1.0]{academic_versus_applied}
    \end{column}
  \end{columns}
  \slidesources{Huyen2022}
\end{frame}

% ----------------------------------------------------------------------
% The punchline of Lecture 0. No \phimg here on purpose: the takeaway is one
% centred red statement and a placeholder box beside it competes with it.
\begin{frame}{}
  \begin{takeaway}
    The algorithm is the small box in the middle.\\
    Everything else is this course.
  \end{takeaway}
\end{frame}

